  "body": "## Change and mathematical scope\n\nThis is a scoped structural result: **no new \u03ba, no construction.** It adds only `research/complex-rank8-rigidity-extension/`. The selected record, `upstream/` and all frozen pins are unchanged.\n\nRank-8 gauge rigidity of the masked (E-graph) system, K = D(2G)D\u207b\u00b9, is known on every P104 frame complement: #1212 (type (2,2,2,2)), #1389 (type (4,4)) and #1399 (admissible). #1399's open next question is the intermediate punctures P105\u2013P110. This PR settles the ones a linear argument reaches and counts exactly what is left.\n\n- **Lemma 10 (extension).** Suppose L \u2282 L\u2032, L is rigid, L spans R\u2078, and for each new line `i` the lines of L orthogonal to `i` span `r_i^\u22a5`. Then L\u2032 is rigid. K\u2032[L,L] already has rank 8, so each new row is `2u\u1d40R\u1d40D\u207b\u00b9` on L. The orthogonal zeros force `u \u221d r_i`, and the diagonal fixes the scale. There is no DRUP, no zero exclusion and no class argument.\n- **Theorem.** E8 \\ S is rigid whenever S lies in the union of two disjoint frames.\n- **Census for P112 minus X:**\n  - It is covered exactly when X lies in a frame inside P112.\n  - Every line, and every orthogonal pair, lies in a type-(2,2,2,2) frame. So **P111 and all 3304 orthogonal-pair P110 units are rigid from #1212 alone**. For P111 this is a second proof of #1389's result, by a different route.\n  - For |X| = 3, \u2026, 7 the covered counts are 29232, 77868, 70560, 35280 and 10080. PROOF.md splits them by which predecessor they need.\n\n**Consequence.** On each covered unit, every decoder with exactly eight costly terms has cst \u2265 72 \u2212 p in the E-graph model, with p = 8 + |X|. This is #968's Theorem 4(b) via #1212 \u00a78.\n\n## Evidence\n\n- `python3 -I -B research/complex-rank8-rigidity-extension/verify.py` runs 9 exact checks plus 5 algebraic checks and controls in about 1 s, standard library only. It has its own E8 model and recomputes:\n  - the 2025 frames and W(E8) transitivity on them;\n  - the three FRAME0-stabiliser orbits of the 1260 P112 frames (28/336/896), matched to their types;\n  - the span condition on all 1260 \u00d7 16 new lines, with at least 52 orthogonal live lines each;\n  - the bi-frame cover census, the P111 cover, the P105\u2013P110 table, and the odd-pair P110 orbits (224 and 2688).\n- Its controls show that both hypotheses of Lemma 10 matter:\n  - a rank-9 solution escapes;\n  - dropping enough lines to break the span condition admits a rank-8 non-gauge extension;\n  - a perturbed orthogonal entry is rejected.\n- Passes on Python 3.10, 3.12 and 3.14. `scripts/research_package.py`: the metadata check and the Python 3.12 registered replay pass. `test_research_*` passes.\n\n## Not checked or not covered\n\n- **Punctures containing an odd pair.** No frame contains an odd pair. This is most of P105\u2013P110. At P110 it is 2912 pairs in two stabiliser orbits, and that is the package's open next question. Each orbit needs its own zero-exclusion certificate.\n- Orthogonal X that do not extend to a frame inside P112, for example 1792 of the 31024 orthogonal triples.\n- **P104 rigidity is cited, not recomputed.** #1212, #1389 and #1399 are all open. P111 and the covered P110 units need only #1212.\n- Nine or more costly terms. #968's premise (F6) is inherited. Attainment of 72 \u2212 p on these units is not checked. There has been no independent human review.\n\n## Task and overlap\n\n- **Task / scope:** `complex-masked-decoder` (scope key `2a6f2c726b78f9ae7d26`), refined to #1399's open next question. Chooser nonce `35f10aeb31e6ffea65d714368cb51223`, track complex-mechanisms (the donor asked to continue this line), priority revision 2.\n- **Heads:** base `bce54fb3f895cbe5cd4c0eb477c3afeed100190f`. Cites #1212 `57099cb5`, #1389 `5480912a` and #1399 `4e1ba3a1`. No code is imported from any of them.\n- **Claim:** https://github.com/CrocSwap/integer-mult-bounds/discussions/1411\n- **Overlap:** no other claim or PR on P105\u2013P110 or on an extension argument. The overlap search was refreshed at 00:33Z (783 open PRs and 143 discussions, complete pagination).\n\n## Credit\n\n- **burntout76 / Brett Halligan (#1212):** the rigidity theorem, (E), the type-(2,2,2,2) units, the transport and \u00a78.\n- **themilkman216 (#968):** the floor 72 \u2212 p.\n- **DreamingOfClouds (#717):** the E-graph model.\n\nSubstantial AI assistance: Claude Opus 5.5 via Claude Code (lemma, verifier, write-up). Submitted by the account holder. Apache-2.0.\n\n**Feedback.** #1399's handoff proposed a per-orbit DRUP route for P105\u2013P110. A five-minute census showed that a linear lemma covers the partial-frame punctures with no certificate at all, and that the real gap is the odd pairs. A quick \"what does the cheapest argument already reach\" census before planning per-unit certificates saved most of the session.\n\n\ud83e\udd16 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n",
